module v2.std.node_reflection

import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }
import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.diagnostic {
  AmbiguousIntent,
  Diagnostic,
  Outcome,
  Unavailable,
  bind_outcome,
  invariant_port_locus,
  outcome_accepted,
  outcome_rejected
}
import v2.std.compilers.lexing { symbol_intern_lexeme, symbol_lexeme }
import v2.std.decl_index {
  DeclFact,
  TypeItem,
  decl_facts_at,
  logical_qualified_name_from_module,
  type_declarer_qualified_names
}
import v2.std.node { Node, Symbol }
import v2.std.node_query { coproduct_arm_key_list_from_node, node_is_coproduct }

// THE HOST TYPE-REFLECTION SURFACE, AND WHY ONE OF ITS TWO SEAMS IS GONE.
//
// coproduct_nullary_inhabitants below is a host seam: its whole body is a self-call,
// answered by the v1 interpreter's live declaration index (roster arm
// v4_bridge.coproduct_nullary_inhabitants). Nothing in the substrate can give it a body,
// because an inhabitant of an arbitrary T is not derivable from anything the caller hands
// it -- the answer is a fact about the running compiler's own source index.
//
// A SECOND HOST SEAM USED TO SIT BESIDE IT AND IS DELETED RATHER THAN RENAMED. It carried the
// spelling `resolve_type_node`, WHICH THE SCOPE-CARRYING FUNCTION BELOW NOW CARRIES -- the name
// is reused across that deletion, so every sentence here about the SEAM is about the thing that
// was removed and never about the function of the same name that replaced it. The seam took a
// BARE type spelling, and its host arm answered by scanning the interpreter's loaded modules
// and returning the FIRST item whose authored name matched -- so a spelling two modules
// declare was answered from whichever load order reached first, with no diagnostic. That is
// the DESIGN section 5 silent wrongness this module now exists to refuse.
//
// The seam is deleted because after the cutover nothing consumed it: the guarded route establishes
// multiplicity and shape from the KEYED declaration read and returns the node that read
// already carries, so it never needs the bare scan, and every other consumer was moved. A
// rename would have kept a bare-spelling host route alive under a new name, which is exactly
// the surviving-X attractor DESIGN section 3 warns about -- the old vocabulary stays
// available and the next question gets answered in it. The host functions it reached,
// eval_resolve_type_node and the type_item_by_name scan beneath it, had no other caller and
// are deleted with it, so the seed shrinks (DESIGN section 7) rather than growing a synonym.
//
// They used to sit in v2.std.node and v2.std.node_query, which the v2 compiler's import
// closure carries. Emission is decided at MODULE grain, so both were emitted into the
// compiler's own Rust crate as panic!("unrealized host seam ..."): a declaration with no
// behaviour on that target, standing in a shipped compiler because rustc accepts a
// diverging body. Neither was reachable from a compile once its one call-reachable
// consumer (target_model's UriScheme node decode) was repointed at a declared inhabitant
// roster, so the residue was module membership and nothing else.
//
// Segregating them here is what makes that provable rather than argued: this module is
// imported only by lenses and witnesses, so a compile-closure declaration that reaches
// reflection again drags THIS module into the closure and the seam reappears in the
// emitted crate -- loudly, at the same grep the census reads. The rule the split states
// is that reflection is an INTERPRETER-time capability: available to a lens or a witness
// reading the live tree, never to a declaration the compiler must emit.
fn coproduct_nullary_inhabitants<T>(
  type_name: Symbol,
  discriminant_of: fn(T) -> Symbol
) -> Outcome<List<T>> {
  coproduct_nullary_inhabitants(
    type_name: type_name,
    discriminant_of: discriminant_of
  )
}

// THE SCOPE-CARRYING ROUTE, AND THE DEFECT IT REPLACED.
//
// THE DELETED SEAM of this name keyed on a BARE spelling. Its host arm (v4_bridge.resolve_type_node,
// realized by v1.stage0.coproduct_reflection type_item_by_name) answered it by scanning the
// interpreter's loaded modules in order and returning the FIRST item whose authored name
// matched. When one spelling is declared as a closed coproduct in two modules, that scan
// returned whichever module the load order reached first -- a different arm set, with no
// diagnostic. It was DESIGN section 5 silent wrongness: not a refusal, a wrong answer that
// typechecks. The corpus inhabits the invalid state today, so this is a live class and not a
// hypothetical one; the discriminating pair enrolled against it is in
// `v2.test.manual.scoped_type_reflection_conformance`.
//
// The fix mints NO new identity and NO new host seam, which is the whole point (DESIGN
// section 3). The multiplicity that decides ambiguity is already a substrate fact:
// `v2.std.decl_index` decl_facts carries one row per declaration with its bare `name`, its
// `kind`, and its `qualified_name`. So the guard is derivable here, in `.dag`, from the index
// that `v2.std.decl_ref_resolution` already treats as the authority for exactly this question
// -- "how many declarations answer to this name" -- and whose five-arm refusal law this module
// deliberately does not restate.
//
// Consumers (DESIGN section 3c), and there is NO open frontier here any more. Consumed by execution
// in this change: v2.lens.affected_set (through the guarded route, with a refusal landing in
// UniverseIncomplete), the generated coproduct witnesses, the reflection conformance witness, the
// wiring-liveness entry, and the conformance claims in
// v2.test.manual.scoped_type_reflection_conformance.
//
// An earlier revision of this annotation classified that cutover as a DECLARED FRONTIER and said the
// bare pair stayed reachable and unguarded until it landed. review 65702 caught that the sentence
// had outlived the tree: the cutover HAD landed in this same change, so the classification was the
// wrong section 3c arm and, worse, it justified leaving a silent-first-match route importable after
// its consumers were gone -- the escape hatch DESIGN section 5 forbids and the surviving-X attractor
// section 3 warns about. The bare pair is now deleted rather than described, so there is nothing
// left for a frontier sentence to be about.
// WHY THIS IS A KEYED READ AND NOT A FOLD OVER decl_facts. The first version of this guard filtered
// the whole declaration corpus, which was correct and cost roughly 3.6s per call against 1ms for a
// keyed read, sharing at no grain -- four calls inside ONE claim frame cost four times one. That put
// the guard that makes reflection sound far above the floor's per-claim ceiling, and it was itself
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode shared_precondition_re_derived_once_per_claim_frame in the very route
// meant to make the reflection memo key sound. v2.std.decl_index type_declarer_qualified_names is
// the keyed answer: the host filters the same memoized pool decl_facts_at reads and marshals no
// declaration nodes, and it returns EVERY candidate rather than choosing among them, so the
// multiplicity and the candidate identities are both decided here.
fn type_declarer_names(pool_roots: List<String>, type_name: Symbol) -> List<String> {
  type_declarer_qualified_names(
    pool_roots: pool_roots,
    bare_name: symbol_lexeme(sym: type_name)
  )
}

fn scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(type_name: Symbol, invariant: Symbol) -> Diagnostic {
  Diagnostic {
    reason: invariant,
    at: invariant_port_locus(port: type_name, invariant: invariant),
    correction: Unavailable { reason: AmbiguousIntent }
  }
}

// THE ONLY PATH TO THE BARE HOST SEAM, SHARED BY BOTH ROUTES ON PURPOSE. review 65571 found that
// the shape check had been added to the spelling route and not to the citation route, so the
// citation route still reached the seam for a record and got an untyped host error -- the exact
// exit the sibling had just closed. Two copies of one acceptance law is the section 3 fork that
// produced that divergence, so there is now ONE copy and neither route may call the seam except
// through it.
//
// It establishes SHAPE after its caller has established uniqueness. decl_facts kind TypeItem does
// not separate a closed coproduct from a record -- this module's own UriScheme specimen depends on
// the v2.std.network RECORD counting as a candidate -- so uniqueness alone was never the contract
// the annotations claimed. No new authority: node_is_coproduct is v2.std.node_query's existing
// predicate, read from the node the keyed fact already carries.
//
// IT READS AT THE GRAIN ITS CALLER MEASURED, which review 65885 caught it not doing. Both callers
// establish multiplicity over a KIND-FILTERED census: type_declarer_qualified_names counts only
// declarations whose kind is TypeItem. decl_facts_at filters on qualified name and NOTHING ELSE, so
// taking its head would answer from whichever row the canonical order happened to put first
// whenever a logical qualified name carries a type declaration AND a non-type one -- the uniqueness
// would have been proven over one population and the answer taken from another, which is the
// first-match-without-a-diagnostic shape this module exists to close, one filter down.
//
// The repair is construction rather than a check: narrow the keyed read to the same kind the guard
// counted, and refuse unless exactly one row survives. There is no arm to test into existence
// because there is no arm that picks.
//
// THE STATE IS CURRENTLY UNPOPULATED AND THAT IS NOT WHY IT IS FIXED. Two declarations share one
// logical qualified name only when logical_qualified_name_from_module strips a leading `v2.` and
// both `v2.X` and `X` declare the name; searched across those twelve colliding module pairs, ZERO
// names are declared as a type on one side and a non-type on the other today. So no RED is
// authorable from the live corpus, and writing a refusal claim against it would be a decoration
// that greens by construction (DESIGN section 4b). The mismatch is removed at the read instead,
// where it cannot be reintroduced by someone later adding the declaration that would populate it.
fn accept_sole_declaration(
  pool_roots: List<String>,
  type_name: Symbol,
  sole_qualified_name: String
) -> Outcome<Node> {
  let at_name = decl_facts_at(pool_roots: pool_roots, qualified_name: sole_qualified_name)
  let type_rows = filter(xs: at_name, predicate: fn(fact) { fact.kind == TypeItem })
  if length(xs: type_rows) > 1 {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
        type_name: type_name,
        invariant: ^cited_type_qualified_name_ambiguous
      )
    )
  } else {
    match list_head(xs: type_rows) {
      HeadAbsent =>
        outcome_rejected(
          d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
            type_name: type_name,
            invariant: ^type_declaration_absent
          )
        )
      HeadFound { value: sole } =>
        if node_is_coproduct(node: sole.node) {
          outcome_accepted(value: sole.node)
        } else {
          outcome_rejected(
            d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
              type_name: type_name,
              invariant: ^type_spelling_is_not_a_closed_coproduct
            )
          )
        }
    }
  }
}

// A bare spelling resolves ONLY where the index proves exactly one type declaration answers to
// it; zero and many each refuse, typed and located, rather than widening to a first match
// (DESIGN section 5 -- a failure arm refuses, never widens). The single-row arm is the only one
// that may reach the bare host seam, and it may do so precisely because uniqueness has been
// established BEFORE the call rather than assumed by it.
fn resolve_type_node(pool_roots: List<String>, type_name: Symbol) -> Outcome<Node> {
  let candidates = type_declarer_names(pool_roots: pool_roots, type_name: type_name)
  let n = length(xs: candidates)
  if n == 0 {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
        type_name: type_name,
        invariant: ^type_declaration_absent
      )
    )
  } else if n > 1 {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
        type_name: type_name,
        invariant: ^type_spelling_ambiguous_across_modules
      )
    )
  } else {
    match list_head(xs: candidates) {
      HeadAbsent =>
        outcome_rejected(
          d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
            type_name: type_name,
            invariant: ^type_declaration_absent
          )
        )
      HeadFound { value: sole_qualified_name } =>
        accept_sole_declaration(
          pool_roots: pool_roots,
          type_name: type_name,
          sole_qualified_name: sole_qualified_name
        )
    }
  }
}

fn coproduct_arm_keys(
  pool_roots: List<String>,
  type_name: Symbol
) -> Outcome<List<Symbol>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: resolve_type_node(pool_roots: pool_roots, type_name: type_name),
    f: fn(node) {
      outcome_accepted(value: coproduct_arm_key_list_from_node(node: node))
    }
  )
}

// THE ARM-KEY READING OF THE CITATION ROUTE, declared because the spelling route has one and a
// caller that cites its module needs the same answer shape. It binds over resolve_type_node_at
// exactly as coproduct_arm_keys binds over resolve_type_node, so the two routes
// differ in how the declaration is NAMED and in nothing else -- no second arm-key law.
// Consumer: the conformance claims in v2.test.manual.scoped_type_reflection_conformance that read
// a forked spelling through each of its declaring modules.
fn coproduct_arm_keys_at(
  pool_roots: List<String>,
  ref: DeclarationRef
) -> Outcome<List<Symbol>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: resolve_type_node_at(pool_roots: pool_roots, ref: ref),
    f: fn(node) {
      outcome_accepted(value: coproduct_arm_key_list_from_node(node: node))
    }
  )
}

// THE ROUTE THAT MAKES A CALLER SAY WHICH MODULE IT MEANT, and the one that can RESOLVE what the
// spelling route can only refuse. `resolve_type_node` refuses an ambiguous spelling because
// a bare name does not say which declaration was meant; a caller that legitimately means one of
// several same-spelled types names it by citation instead, reusing `std.decl_ref` DeclarationRef --
// the corpus's existing carrier for "this declaration, in this module" -- so no second spelling of a
// scoped type identity is minted here. When PR #11264 lands its occurrence bindings, this is the
// declaration whose parameter becomes that binding: the citation is refined in place, not forked.
//
// IT REFUSES MULTIPLICITY AT ITS OWN GRAIN TOO, which review 65678 caught me missing. Removing the
// bare-uniqueness conjunct was right; leaving the ZERO case as the only refusal was not, because it
// sent every non-zero multiplicity into the accept path, and that path takes the head of the keyed
// read. Two declarations under ONE logical qualified name would then have been answered from
// whichever row came first -- the same first-match-without-a-diagnostic shape this module exists to
// close, moved one grain down from bare spelling to qualified name. This module's own seed-growth
// receipt states the law that forbids it: two rows under one logical qualified name is an ambiguous
// citation, and collapsing it silently RESOLVES one, which is a wrong answer and not a cost defect.
//
// IT ASKS ONLY WHETHER THE CITED NAME RESOLVES, and review 65649 is why that is worth stating. An
// earlier revision also required the BARE spelling to be globally unique, and refused otherwise on
// an invariant naming the bare host seam. That prohibition was true when the accept path still
// delegated to the seam, which could not be told which module to read -- and it outlived its own
// premise by one commit: once `accept_sole_declaration` began returning the node the KEYED read
// already carries, nothing in this module consults load order, so a qualified key is exact and a
// cited type in a forked spelling is perfectly resolvable. Keeping the conjunct would have forbidden
// more than the evidence supports (DESIGN section 4d) and would have made the citation route unable
// to serve the only case it exists for.
fn resolve_type_node_at(pool_roots: List<String>, ref: DeclarationRef) -> Outcome<Node> {
  let wanted = logical_qualified_name_from_module(
    module_name: ref.module_path as String,
    name: ref.decl_name as String
  )
  let type_name = symbol_intern_lexeme(lexeme: ref.decl_name as String)
  let candidates = type_declarer_names(pool_roots: pool_roots, type_name: type_name)
  let scoped = filter(xs: candidates, predicate: fn(qn) { qn == wanted })
  if length(xs: scoped) == 0 {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
        type_name: type_name,
        invariant: ^cited_type_declaration_absent
      )
    )
  } else if length(xs: scoped) > 1 {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: scoped_type_reflection_diagnostic(
        type_name: type_name,
        invariant: ^cited_type_qualified_name_ambiguous
      )
    )
  } else {
    accept_sole_declaration(
      pool_roots: pool_roots,
      type_name: type_name,
      sole_qualified_name: wanted
    )
  }
}
